is distinct from the non\u2011sunny ones, so the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and the required number of sunny lines is attained.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points on a sunny line).\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Write its equation in reduced form  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p}{q}x + r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  \nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\) are distinct, then  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)= (q,p) .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) differ by a vector parallel to the direction vector \\((q,p)\\) of the line; this follows from the reduced slope representation.  \nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) the sum of the absolute changes of the coordinates of any two points cannot exceed \\(n\\) (because moving from one point to another cannot increase the sum \\(a+b\\) by more than \\(n\\)). Hence \\(|p|+|q|\\) times the number of steps is at most \\(n\\), giving (1). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points on a non\u2011sunny line).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly \\(\\min\\{d-1,n+1-d\\}\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines).\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k + n\\,(n-k) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points (the bound (1) gives at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points, and for \\(n\\ge5\\) this is \\(\\le2\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSumming over the \\(k\\) sunny and the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines yields (2). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Non\u2011existence of sunny lines for \\(n\\ge5\\)).\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge5\\) any covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines must have \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2 -k(n-2)\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} < 1 ,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Existence of a covering with no sunny line).\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Existence of a covering with exactly one sunny line for \\(n=3,4\\)).\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) already cover all points of \\(S_3\\). Adding any sunny line, for instance the line through \\((2,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\), yields a family of three distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Case \\(n=4\\).*  \nThe three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) cover all points of \\(S_4\\). Adding any sunny line (e.g. the line through \\((2,1)\\) with slope \\(3\\)) gives a family of four distinct lines with exactly one sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (Lemma\u202f5).  \n* For \\(n=3,4\\) the value \\(k=1\\) is attainable (Lemma\u202f6).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge5\\) no sunny line can appear in any admissible configuration (Lemma\u202f4).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0\\},   & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
