r of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the slope is \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms, the vector \\((q,p)\\) is the smallest integer vector parallel to \\(\\ell\\); any two lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) differ by an integer multiple of \\((q,p)\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) the sum of the absolute changes of the coordinates between any two points cannot exceed \\(n\\) (otherwise the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) would be violated).  Hence \\(|p|+|q|\\) times the number of steps is at most \\(n\\), giving (1). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Points on a non\u2011sunny line).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) contains the points \\((a,c)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) is analogous and also contains at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) contains the points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and \\(d-a\\ge1\\); the number of such points is \\(\\min\\{d-1,n+1-d\\}\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Necessary bound for the number of sunny lines).\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nUsing Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\;\\le\\;\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;+\\;(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2) for \\(k\\) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\bigl(n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr)}=\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{n+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor=n-2, & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus (3) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Sufficiency of the bound).\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nThe four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are exhibited in the table of \u00a75; all satisfy (3) (for \\(n=3\\) the bound (3) gives \\(k\\le3\\)).\n\n**Base \\(n=4\\).**  \nThe table of \u00a75 shows that \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are attainable; (3) gives \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (3) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 use a horizontal line.*  \nTake the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); it contains exactly \\(n+1\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \nRemoving these points leaves the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}'=\\{(a,b-1)\\mid (a,b)\\in S_{n+1},\\;b\\ge2\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely a translate of \\(S_n\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis we can cover \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\) lines containing any admissible number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines.  \nChoosing \\(k'=k\\) (which is allowed because the bound for \\(n\\) is at least as large as the bound for \\(n+1\\) minus one) gives a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines; the newly added horizontal line is non\u2011sunny.\n\nIf the chosen \\(k\\) is **strictly larger** than the maximal admissible number for \\(n\\) (this can happen only when \\(n\\) is odd, because the bound jumps from \\(n-2\\) to \\(n-1\\) when passing from an odd to the next even integer), we must obtain one extra sunny line.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 create one extra sunny line.*  \nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, the bound (3) for \\(n+1\\) equals \\(n\\) (since \\(n+1\\) is even) whereas the bound for \\(n\\) equals \\(n-2\\).  \nSelect a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) that passes through the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,\\,c+1),\\;(2,\\,c+2),\\dots,\\;\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\;c+\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nwith a suitable integer \\(c\\) so that all these points belong to \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \nSuch a line contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) (the maximal number allowed by Lemma\u202f1).  \n\nDelete those points.  The remaining points split into two parts:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (still present);\n* the translate of \\(S_{n-1}\\) obtained by shifting the original triangle down by one unit and removing the points already covered by the sunny line.\n\nBy the induction hypothesis applied to \\(n-1\\) we can cover the translate of \\(S_{n-1}\\) with \\(n-1\\) lines containing any admissible number of sunny lines up to the bound for \\(n-1\\), which is at least \\(k-1\\).  \nAdding the sunny line of slope\u202f1 and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) yields a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus in both sub\u2011cases we have produced a configuration satisfying the required parameters.  This completes the induction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\)).\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f3 (necessity) and Lemma\u202f4 (sufficiency) we obtain:\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even (\\(n\\ge4\\)), the admissible values are  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd (\\(n\\ge5\\)), the admissible values are  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2 .\n  \\]\n\n* For the exceptional case \\(n=3\\) all four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur (see \u00a75).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Explicit examples for the small cases \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nn & \\text{admissible }k\\text{ and a concrete family of }n\\text{ lines}\\\\ \\hline\n3 &\n\\begin{array}{l}\nk=0:\\; y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x=1;\\\\\nk=1:\\; x=1,\\;y=1,\\;\\text{sunny line through }(2,1)\\text{ slope }2;\\\\\nk=2:\\; \\text{sunny line through }(1,1)\\text{ slope }2,\\;\n      \\text{sunny line through }(1,2)\\text{ slope }3,\\;\n      x=2;\\\\\nk=3:\\; \\text{three distinct sunny lines, each passing through one of }(1,1),(1,2),(2,1).\n\\end{array}\\\\ \\hline\n4 &\n\\begin{array}{l}\nk=0:\\; y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3,\\;y=4;\\\\\nk=1:\\; x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4,\\;\\text{sunny line through }(2,1)\\text{ slope }3;\\\\\nk=2:\\; \\text{sunny lines through }(1,1)\\text{ slope }2\\text{ and }(1,2)\\text{ slope }3,\\\\\n\\qquad\\text{together with }y=1,\\;y=2;\\\\\nk=3:\\; \\text{sunny lines through }(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\text{ with slopes }2,3,4,\\\\\n\\qquad\\text{plus the horizontal line }y=1.\n\\end{array}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary inequality (3) together with the inductive construction shows that the bound is also sufficient.  Hence the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the theorem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
