lus a sunny line through \\((2,2)\\).  \n   - \\(n=4\\): explicit families for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are given in the table below.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (3) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n   - **Keeping the same number of sunny lines.**  \n     Add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n     Removing the points on this line leaves a translate of \\(S_n\\); by the induction hypothesis we can cover it with \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n     Thus we obtain a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Increasing the number of sunny lines by one** (this situation occurs only when \\(n\\) is odd, because then the bound jumps from \\(n-2\\) for \\(n\\) to \\(n-1\\) for \\(n+1\\)).  \n     Choose a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) that passes through the points  \n\n     \\[\n     (1,\\,c+1),\\;(2,\\,c+2),\\dots,\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\;c+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     \\]\n\n     with a suitable integer \\(c\\) so that all of them belong to \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n     This line contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) – the maximal number allowed by (1).  \n     Delete those points.  The remaining set splits into  \n\n     * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (still present), and  \n     * a translate of \\(S_{n-1}\\).  \n\n     By the induction hypothesis applied to \\(n-1\\) we can cover the translate of \\(S_{n-1}\\) with \\(n-1\\) lines containing any admissible number of sunny lines up to the bound for \\(n-1\\), in particular \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \n     Adding the chosen sunny line and the horizontal line yields a covering of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Thus every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) can be realized.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary inequality (3) and the constructive induction together give the exact set of admissible numbers of