\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q\\neq0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\]\n\nIf two lattice points of \\(S_{n}\\) lie on this line, their difference is a non‑zero integer multiple of the vector \\((q,p)\\).  \nConsequently the sum of the absolute changes of the two coordinates between any two points of the line is at least \\(|p|+|q|\\).  \nSince for any two points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S_{n}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|a_{1}-a_{2}|+|b_{1}-b_{2}|\\le n ,\n\\]\n\nthe line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_{n}\\).  (The inequality \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) follows from \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\).)\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nSuch a line meets the triangle \\(S_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points; for the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) the maximum is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\).\n\nNow let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nUsing the maximal capacities we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.2) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\