h the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-3\\,\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The three “boundary’’ subsets of \\(S_{n}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \n   B=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad   \n   C=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   *A sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).*  \n   (If the line has reduced slope \\(p/q\\) with \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\), the vector \\((q,p)\\) moves at least two units in the Manhattan metric; the triangle \\(S_{n}\\) has width \\(n\\), hence at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) lattice points lie on it.)\n\n   *A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).*  \n   (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines intersect the triangle in a segment of length at most \\(n\\).)\n\n   Consequently, for a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-2, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) is not tight because of the three boundary sets \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n   Each sunny line meets each of \\(A,B,C\\) in **at most one** point (otherwise it would be parallel to the corresponding side).  \n   Since \\(|A|=|B|=|C|=n\\), to cover a whole boundary set we need either a non‑sunny line of the appropriate direction or at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, which is impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\n   Therefore each boundary set requires **at least one** non‑sunny line.  \n   For **even** \\(n\\) the three sets are pairwise disjoint, so we need at least three non‑sunny lines; hence  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-3 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For **odd** \\(n\\) the diagonal \\(C\\) shares a point with each of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); a single diagonal non‑sunny line can serve for \\(C\\) and simultaneously contribute one point to \\(A\\) and one to \\(B\\).  Consequently only two non‑sunny lines are necessary, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (4) with the trivial lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) yields the necessary conditions stated in the final answer.\n\n3. **Sufficiency (construction).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying the above bounds can be realised.\n\n   *Base cases.*  \n\n   - \\(n=3\\) (odd).  \\(S_{3}\\) has six points.  \n     • \\(k=0\\): three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n     • \\(k=1\\): lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=2x-2\\) (through \\((2,2)\\)).  \n\n   - \\(n=4\\) (even).  \\(S_{4}\\) has ten points.  \n     • \\(k=0\\): four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n     • \\(k=1\\): lines \\(y=1, y=2, y=3\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=2x-2\\) (through \\((2,2)\\)).  \n     No configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists, in agreement with the bound \\(k\\le n-3=1\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for all integers \\(m<n\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound for the given \\(n\\).\n\n   - **Odd \\(n\\).**  \n     Use the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     \\(H\\) covers the whole set \\(B\\); \\(D\\) covers the whole set \\(C\\) and also the points \\((1,n)\\in A\\) and \\((n,1)\\in B\\).  \n     Remove from \\(S_{n}\\) the points already covered by \\(H\\cup D\\); the remaining set is exactly a translate of \\(S_{n-2}\\) (subtract \\((1,1)\\) from every remaining point).  \n     By the induction hypothesis there exists a family of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines covering this translate with any prescribed number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k'\\le (n-2)-2=n-4\\).  \n     Choose \\(k'=k\\) (possible because the bound for odd \\(n\\) gives \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n     The final family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H,D\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{n-2,k}\n     \\]\n\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covers all of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   - **Even \\(n\\).**  \n     Use the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     \\(H\\) covers \\(B\\), \\(V\\) covers \\(A\\), and \\(D\\) covers \\(C\\).  \n     After deleting the points of these three lines, the leftover set is a translate of \\(S_{n-3}\\) (subtract \\((1,1)\\) from each).  \n     By the induction hypothesis there is a family of \\(n-3\\) distinct lines covering this translate with any number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k'\\le (n-3)-2=n-5\\).  \n     Because for even \\(n\\) the admissible range is \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), we set \\(k'=k\\) (which is allowed).  \n     The final family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H,V,D\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{n-3,k}\n     \\]\n\n     has \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) sunny, and covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   The inductive constructions work for every admissible \\(k\\); the