\nIts intersection with the triangle is a segment of length at most \\(n\\); consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A non‑sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_{n}.}\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.3  Global inequality  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \nUsing (1.1) and (1.2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\nSolving (1.3) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-2, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1.4}\n\\]\n\n#### 1.4  Boundary‑set argument  \n\nEach sunny line meets each of the three boundary sets \\(A,B,C\\) in **at most one** point; otherwise it would be parallel to the corresponding side.  \nSince \\(|A|=|B|=|C|=n\\), a whole boundary set cannot be covered solely by sunny lines (that would require at least \\(n\\) sunny lines).  \nHence every boundary set needs at least one non‑sunny line.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, the three sets are pairwise disjoint, so we need at least three non‑sunny lines.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{1.5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, the diagonal \\(C\\) shares the point \\((1,n)\\) with \\(A\\) and the point \\((n,1)\\) with \\(B\\).  \nA single diagonal non‑sunny line therefore contributes to two boundary sets simultaneously, and only two non‑sunny lines are necessary.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1.6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1.5) and (1.6) with the obvious lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) yields the **necessary** condition\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{1.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructive sufficiency\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1.7) can be realised.\n\n#### 2.1  Base cases  \n\n* \\(n=3\\) (odd).  \n  - \\(k=0\\): the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n  - \\(k=1\\): the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,2\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-2\\) (through \\((2,2)\\)).  \n\n* \\(n=4\\) (even).  \n  - \\(k=0\\): the four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n  - \\(k=1\\): the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-2\\).  \n\nThese families contain exactly the required number of sunny lines and cover all points of \\(S_{3}\\) and \\(S_{4}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2.1  Odd \\(n\\)\n\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) covers the whole set \\(B\\); \\(D\\) covers the whole set \\(C\\) and also the points \\((1,n)\\in A\\) and \\((n,1)\\in B\\).  \nRemove from \\(S_{n}\\) the points lying on \\(H\\cup D\\).  \nThe remaining points are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\n   =\\{(a-1,b-1)\\mid (a,b)\\in S_{n-2}\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a translate of \\(S_{n-2}\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there exists, for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le (n-2)-2=n-4\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2,k'}\\) of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines covering this translate and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nChoose \\(k'=k\\) (possible because for odd \\(n\\) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H,D\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{n-2,k}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2.2  Even \\(n\\)\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad V:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) covers \\(B\\), \\(V\\) covers \\(A\\), and \\(D\\) covers \\(C\\).  \nAfter deleting the points of \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n-1\\}\n   =\\{(a-1,b-1)\\mid (a,b)\\in S_{n-3}\\},\n\\]\n\na translate of \\(S_{n-3}\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, for every integer \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le (n-3)-2=n-5\\) there is a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3,k'}\\) of \\(n-3\\) distinct lines covering this translate and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nSet \\(k'=k\\) (allowed because for even \\(n\\) we have \\(k\\le n-3\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{H,V,D\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{n-3,k}.\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThe inductive constructions never reuse a line of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) as a sunny line, so the count of sunny lines is correct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (1.7) and the inductive constructions together show that it is also sufficient.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-3\\,\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n* for \\(n=3\\) the possible values are \\(k=0,1\\);  \n* for \\(n=4\\) the possible values are \\(k=0,1\\);  \n* for every odd \\(n\\ge5\\) all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) occur;  \n* for every even \\(n\\ge6\\) all integers \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) occur.  \n\nThus the problem is completely solved.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 sma