ying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ccl}\n\\{0,1\\}                     & & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}                     & & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}                     & & n=5,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,n-3\\}                 & & n\\ge 6 .\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   The three “border’’ subsets are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \n   B=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad   \n   C=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Three non‑sunny lines are unavoidable for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Any two points of \\(A\\) (resp. \\(B\\), \\(C\\)) determine a vertical (resp. horizontal, slope \\(-1\\)) line, which is non‑sunny.  \n   A sunny line can meet each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\) in **at most one point**; therefore, to cover all points of the three sides we need at least one non‑sunny line for each side.  \n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) each side contains at least four points, so the three non‑sunny lines are forced.  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the side \\(C\\) has only three points; two non‑sunny lines together with one sunny line already cover the whole set, hence the lower bound drops to two.\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-k\\ge 3 & (n\\ge4),\\\\[2mm]\n   n-k\\ge 2 & (n=3).\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The interior triangle.**  \n   After fixing the three boundary lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) the uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\qq