cond term is omitted; the formula still gives an upper bound.)\n\n   Requiring this upper bound to be at least \\(|R_n|=2n-5\\) yields\n\n   \\[\n   2(n-2)+(n-5-k)(n-3)+k\\;\\ge\\;2n-5 .\n   \\]\n\n   After simplification we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   (n-4)(\\,n-3-k\\,)\\;\\ge\\;0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n\\ge6\\) we have \\(n-4>0\\); therefore the inequality forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,n-3\\}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points.  \n   The three boundary lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) already cover all of them; we may replace any one of the three lines by a sunny line that passes through a single point (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \n   Hence the admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\) and \\(n=5\\).*  \n   For these two values the inequality (5) gives the same restriction \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).  \n   Direct constructions (given in §7) show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable.\n\n7. **Explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the three boundary non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) and the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny and together they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Keep the three boundary lines.  \n   Replace the vertical line \\(x=2\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x-2\\) (which passes through \\((2,2)\\)).  \n   The remaining vertical lines \\(x=3,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the three boundary lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=n-3\\) (possible for \\(n\\ge6\\)).*  \n   Again start with the three boundary non‑sunny lines.  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-4}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le c\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-4}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\]\n\n   Inside the interior triangle \\(T_n\\) the li