 $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n-1,\\;n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.*  \n   Denote by  \n   \\[\n   V=\\{x=a\\},\\qquad H=\\{y=b\\},\\qquad D=\\{x+y=c\\}\n   \\]\n   the families of vertical, horizontal and diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) lines.\n\n2. **Upper bound for the number of points that a sunny line can meet.**  \n   A sunny line meets each line of the families \\(V,H,D\\) in at most one point; consequently it meets each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.  \n\n3. **Counting points that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(v,h,d\\) be the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines that are used; the remaining \\(k=n-(v+h+d)\\) lines are sunny.  \n   The points that are **not** on any of the \\(v+h+d\\) non‑sunny lines are exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\notin A,\\;b\\notin B,\\;a+b\\notin C,\n   \\]\n   where \\(A,B,C\\) are the sets of selected columns, rows and diagonals.  \n   A simple extremal argument shows that, whatever the choice of \\(A,B,C\\) with \\(|A|+|B|+|C|=v+h+d=n-k\\), the number of such points is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, the worst case is obtained by taking only vertical lines; then the uncovered columns are the last \\(k\\) ones and they contain exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\) lattice points.)\n\n4. **Capacity of sunny lines.**  \n   Because a sunny line meets each diagonal at most once, the total number of lattice points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combining (1) and (2).**  \n   The uncovered points must be covered by sunny lines, hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k n .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k\\ge n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore no configuration exists for \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Construction for the four admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All points are on a vertical line, and no line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one additional sunny line\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1),\n   \\]\n   which passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not covered by the vertical lines.  \n   Thus exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Choose the single non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (the longest diagonal).  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-(i-1)x+i .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) contains the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\); together the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(L_i\\) cover every lattice point whose column is different from the column of the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Hence exactly \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-(i-1)x+i .\n   \\]\n\n   As above, \\(L_i\\) contains the points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) together contain every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   All four constructions satisfy the required covering condition, and the numbers of sunny lines are respectively \\(0,1,n-1,n\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (3) no other value of \\(k\\) is possible, and the four values exhibited above are attainable. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a\\;(1\\le a\\le n),\\qquad\nH_b:\\;y=b\\;(1\\le b\\le n),\\qquad\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1)\n\\]\n\nthe three families of non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the points that must be covered by sunny lines  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines we have  \n\n\\[\nv\\; \\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh\\; \\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd\\; \\text{diagonal lines},\n\\qquad\nk=n-(v+h+d) \\; \\text{sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{a\\mid V_a\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nB=\\{b\\mid H_b\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nC=\\{c\\mid D_c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is **uncovered by the non‑sunny lines** iff  \n\n\\[\na\\notin A,\\; b\\notin B,\\; a+b\\notin C .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe claim that the number of points satisfying (1) is at least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\nIndeed, delete all horizontal and diagonal lines (they can only increase the set of uncovered points) and keep only the \\(v\\) vertical lines.  \nChoosing the \\(v\\) smallest possible columns, i.e. \\(A=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\}\\), maximises the number of points covered by vertical lines.  \nThe uncovered columns are then \\(\\{v+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); their lengths are  \n\n\\[\nn,\\;n-1,\\dots ,n-(k-1)=k,\\;k-1,\\dots ,1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\n1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince any other choice of \\(A,B,C\\) can only increase the set of uncovered points, the bound holds for the original configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can the sunny lines cover?  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any line of the families \\(V,H,D\\); consequently it meets each diagonal \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.  \nBecause there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, the total number of lattice points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Consequence  \n\nAll points that satisfy (1) have to be covered by the sunny lines, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(k>0\\) (the case \\(k=0\\) is already known to be possible) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k+1}{2}\\le n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le 1\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration exists for  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions for the four admissible values  \n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines realising the four values of \\(k\\) allowed by (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i : x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)  \n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand one additional sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=-(n-2)x+(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not covered by the vertical